    background: "0,0,100",
    title: "I.2",
    pivot: "D",
    elements: [
        "A;point;free;150,190",
        "B;point;free;180,160",
        "C;point;free;170,90",
        "BC;line;connect;B,C;0;0;blue",
        "ABcirc;circle;radius;A,B",
        "BAcirc;circle;radius;B,A",
        "D'D;line;bichord;ABcirc,BAcirc;0;0;0",
        "D;point;last;D'D;black;green",
        "DA;line;connect;D,A",
        "DB;line;connect;D,B",
        "BCcirc;circle;radius;B,C",
        "G;point;extend;D,B,B,C",
        "BG;line;connect;B,G;0;0;blue",
        "DGcirc;circle;radius;D,G",
        "L;point;extend;D,A,B,C",
        "AL;line;connect;A,L;0;0;blue",
        "LE;line;extend;A,L,A,B",
        "GF;line;extend;B,G,A,B",
        // Invisible highlight targets for the guide prose: {ABD} and
        // {AB} draw nothing normally but render in the gold highlight
        // stroke on hover/tap (the Zeno pattern from propI.1). The
        // side AB is deliberately not drawn in this figure — the
        // prose point is that it isn't needed — so the ref outlines
        // it only on demand.
        "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
        "ABD;polygon;triangle;A,B,D;0;0;0;0"
    ],
});
</script>
</figure>

#### Use of Proposition 2

The construction in this proposition is only used in Proposition @I.3.

Note that this construction assumes that all the point *A* and the line *BC* lie in a plane. It may also be used in space, however, since Proposition @XI.2 implies that *A* and *BC* do lie in a plane.
